05 Apr 26
I've done a load of manual calculations and am ready to
establish an algorithm for finding primes which start a
Cunningham Chains of at least length m
If m is 1, then the answer to this trivial case is the
list of prime numbers.  A different way to express this is
to say, return the odd numbers that aren't composite.
If m is 2, then we exclude prime numbers that are
equivalent to 1 (modulo 6) and only consider the primes
equivalent to 5 (modulo 6).  Further if we consider the
values 6k-1, then we can sieve out values according to
whether the values 6k-1 or 12k-1 are composite.  An
algorithm that works here is to start with k=1 and if 6k-1
or 12k-1 are both prime then this is a value of k that
provides a Cunningham chain of length 2. Iterate to the
next value of k=k+1.  If one or other of 6k-1 or 12k-1 is
composite, then determine the prime factors, p,of the
composite values and then consider the values of k, for
general values of k, such that 6k-1 or 12k-1 are divisible
by p.  This can be done in a general way by considering
the values 6k.2^n where n in this case equals 0 or 1.  If
6k or 6k.2 is equal to 1 modulo p then one of 6k-1 or
12k-1 is divisible by p.  These values of k where p
divides 6k-1 or 12k-1 can be removed from consideration
for future values of k to be considered.
Let’s work an example:
Let k=1 then 6k-1 and 12k-1 are 5 and 11, both prime
Iterate on k, so k=2, 11 and 23 are both prime
k=3, 17 is prime but 35=5.7
So consider prime factors 5 and 7.
For p=5, when k=1 (mod 5) then 6k=1 (mod 5). When k=3 (mod
5) then 12k=1 (mod 5).
For p=7, when k=6 (mod 7) then 6k=1 (mod 7).
When k=3 (mod 7) then 12k=1 (mod 7).
Move to the next value of k, k=4, 23 and 47 are prime
k=5, 29 and 59 are prime
k=6 is removed from consideration since 6=1 (mod 5) and
6=6 (mod 7)
k=7, 41 and 83 are prime
k=8 is removed since 8=3 (mod 5)
k=9, 53 and 107 are prime
k=10 removed (=3 (mod 7))
k=11 removed (=1 (mod 5)
k=12, 71 is prime but 143=11.13

There are some refinements to this algorithm.
For each Cunningham chain investigated from each value of
k, the length of the chain should be checked past the
value of m so that checking of length of chains can be
recorded. The further elements of chains can be
considered. For example for k=1 above the length of the
chain being m=2 length can be considered, ie. The chain a
5, 11 and continues 23, 47, and finishes there as the next
value 95=5.19
Also the elements of this chain also complete the
investigation for later values of k so the work needn’t be
fine for k=2, 4, 8
For k=5 the chain starts 29, 59 then the next value
119=7.17 is composite
k=7, the chain proceeds 41, 83 (k=14), 167 (k=28),
335=5.67 (k=56)
k=9, chain proceeds 53, 107 (k=18), 215=5.43 (k=36)
This implied keeping a list of k values which don't need
to be considered since the Cunningham chain has already
been determined.

A further refinement is to make a note of the primes that have been
encountered in the Cunningham chains, also the factors of
the end-of-chain composite values and add these to sets of
k values to be sieved.

Cunningham chain script proposal
Create a list of candidate primes which could start chains of a
certain length
The script is based on analysis of 30k.2^n-1 values based on the
residues of 30k.2^n modulo p and noting that for primes where 2
is a primitive root, then when p does not divide 30k.2^n then the
values of 30k.2^n form a cycle of residues with period p-1 and
when 30k.2^n is equivalent to 1 (mod p) then the 30k.2^n-1 value
is divisible by p, so the Cunningham chain then ends.

Order (mod 2) p
2	3
3	7
4	5
5	31
6	-
7	127
8	17
9	73
10	11
11	23 89
12	13
..

Every prime can generate a Cunningham chain of length 1
Which primes are excluded for chains of length 2, 3, ..?
When p=1 (mod 6), then 2p+1=3 (mod 6) and is composite
All primes p=1 (mod 6) are excluded as candidates for CC of
length 2

For CC_1 of length 3, consider the values p=5 (mod 6) or
alternatively 6k.2^n-1
If we consider the sequence 6k.2^n (mod 7) then the CC_1 that
6k-1 can form will be at most length 2
If we consider 6k (mod 5) noting that 2,4,3,1 is the cycle of
powers of 2 (mod 5), then if 6k=4,3,1 (mod 5) then the CC_1
length can't be 3

For chains of length 4, then only if 6k (mod 5) = 0 will a chain
of length 4 be possible
So we can restrict ourselves to considering 30.2^n values
As k increases we will want to consider other primes

Return to CC_1 of length 3
